235 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 167 0 R
/T (Federal Ethics Committee's Role in Ethiopian Medical Malpractice Investigations by Yishak Baraki \(yishakdesta495@gmail.com\) )
/E (Federal Ethics Committee's Role in Ethiopian Medical Malpractice Investigations by Yishak Baraki \(yishakdesta495@gmail.com\) )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [1 598 0 R 3]
>>
endobj
236 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Span
/K [2]
/Pg 13 0 R
/P 598 0 R
>>
endobj
237 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 167 0 R
/E (Medical malpractice also referred to as medical negligence or medical error is a growing public health issue among healthcare professionals globally. The most comprehensive definition of medical negligence is 'an act of omission or commission during the planning or execution of medical care that contributes or could contribute to an unintended outcome. In the undeveloped healthcare system of Ethiopia medical malpractice is an issue worth in-depth discussions and analysis. The aim of this a8rticle is to address the role of the Federal Ethics Committee for Health Professionals in Ethiopian medical malpractice cases.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
238 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 167 0 R
/E (The Ethiopian Legal System addresses medical malpractice cases under general tort law and criminal law. When a criminal litigation involving medical malpractice arises it follows the general procedures for criminal litigation with the added step of involving the Federal Ethics Committee for Health Professionals. Criminal litigation of medical malpractice cases in Ethiopia faces multiple challenges. Firstly, the burden of proof is significantly higher with the requirement of proving beyond reasonable doubt.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
239 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 167 0 R
/E (The burden of proof under the preponderance of evidence standard requires the plaintiff to demonstrate that it is more likely than not that malpractice occurred, making it relatively easier for plaintiffs to succeed in their claims. However, proving beyond a reasonable doubt requires a significantly higher burden of proof, demanding that the plaintiff establish with near certainty that malpractice occurred. This higher standard makes it much more challenging for injured parties or plaintiffs to prevail in their claims. Additionally, there is a lack of specialized legal expertise and forensic capabilities to adequately investigate and prosecute these cases.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
240 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 167 0 R
/E (The Federal Ethics Committee for Health Professionals is responsible for investigating complaints against healthcare professionals related to medical malpractice. It reviews cases and determines if there is a breach of ethical conduct or negligence. If misconduct is found, the committee imposes appropriate sanctions, such as license suspension or revocation. The committee may also provide recommendations to the referring authorities if any.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
241 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 167 0 R
/T (| PAGE 2)
/E (| PAGE 2)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
242 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 168 0 R
/Pg 14 0 R
/A [921 0 R 0]
/K [0]
>>
endobj
243 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 168 0 R
/E (There are criticisms regarding the potential bias and effectiveness of the committee. A study found that around 76% of complaints were dismissed on the ground that they were unfounded, this indicates the possible reluctance of the committee to hold health professionals accountable. This situation is referred to as Regulatory Capture which is a situation in which a regulatory body charged with acting in the interest of the public prioritizes the interests of the industry it was charged with regulating. This issue along with cultural reluctance to report malpractice and systemic barriers that result in inadequate medical record keeping hinder the effectiveness of malpractice investigations. Furthermore, the committee and the broader legal and healthcare systems suffer from limited training and resources leading to adverse effects on the investigations.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
244 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 168 0 R
/E (The opinions and actions of the ethics committee have a profound impact on the outcomes of criminal litigation cases. There is a concerning tendency where the authorities seem to be overly reliant on waiting for and deferring to the committee's assessments before even initiating investigations, even in situations where the mistakes made by healthcare providers are obvious)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
245 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 168 0 R
/E (Additionally, the decisions made by the organs of justice almost completely hinge on the findings presented by the committee. This heavy reliance can lead to unjust outcomes in cases where the committee's own shortcomings, such as potential biases or lack of thoroughness in their investigations, come into play. In some instances, the committee's reports are criticized as being rather superficial and lacking the necessary details, yet these inadequate findings are still used as the foundation for crucial judicial decisions.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
246 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 168 0 R
/E (This dependency on incomplete or flawed reports from the committee can worsen the injustice faced by victims, as critical errors or instances of negligence may go unaddressed due to the committee's insufficient investigation or potential biases. Moreover, the immense influence wielded by the committee can undermine the independent assessment and scrutiny by the judiciary, making it extremely difficult to challenge or scrutinize the committee's findings. Healthcare professionals and the public deserve a system where the ethical oversight committee operates with rigor, impartiality, and accountability, rather than having their decisions serve as an unquestionable basis for the courts.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
247 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 168 0 R
/T (| PAGE 3 )
/E (| PAGE 3 )
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
