/P 172 0 R
/E (There are two main types of technology transfer: vertical technology transfer \(from research to development to production\) and horizontal technology transfer \(established technology transferred between operational environments\). Examples include the transfer of technology from the United States to Japan and other countries in the 1950s and 1960s.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
279 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 172 0 R
/E (Formal technology transfer mechanisms include importing capital goods, using patent documents/standards, joint ventures, assignments, licensing, franchising, exchange of technical personnel, and turnkey projects. Informal mechanisms include giving away technology through technical journals, conferences, emigration of technical experts, industrial intelligence, and industrial internships. Overall, technology transfer is essential for enhancing productivity and economic development by sharing and utilizing technological advancements across countries and organizations.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
280 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 172 0 R
/E (A Patent of Introduction in Ethiopia allows for the introduction of a foreign patented invention that has not been patented in Ethiopia. The interested party must declare responsibility for the patent and meet the same requirements as a regular patent application. Additionally, the applicant must provide details of the foreign patent or the information source if unknown. The duration of a Patent of Introduction can be up to ten years. The owner must demonstrate the working of the invention annually from the third year after grant and pay the relevant annual fees. This mechanism aims to promote the adoption, utilization, improvement, and growth of technology in Ethiopia.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
281 0 obj
<<
