endobj
368 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 176 0 R
/E (As per Research and Conservation of Cultural Heritage Proclamation No. 209/2000 Article 14\(1\) cultural heritage may be owned by the state and any person, but a person who owns a historically significant building may not be at liberty to interfere with its architectural features or demolish it including the state authorities. This shows us that greater limitations are to be placed on objects of historical, cultural and sentimental value than ordinary objects. Violating these limitations comes with a criminal consequence because as per Articles 669/1 and 681/2 of the Criminal Code which protect objects with historical, archeological, scientific value make it an aggravating circumstance. These objects are owned by the nation as a whole and are seen as not commodities but as objects of special character. )
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
