487 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 188 0 R
/E (Nowadays, new proclamations and regulations are being introduced on a daily basis to the point where they are becoming one part of our life. Among these, the draft bill to revise court fees in which the Federal Supreme Court proposed, received an approval from the House of Peoples Representatives on November 29,2024[1]. The legislation has increased the court fee on users up to 500%. While updating the outdated court fee law seems to be reasonable, it raises a serious concern whether or not it shuts the door of courthouses for those already struggling financially. This article will discuss the historical background of court fees, the rationale behind the new law, and its implications. Ethiopia has been operating under court fee law dating back to the 1950s which remained unchanged for seven decades despite the change in legal and economic landscape. The court fee Proclamation, no. 177/1945 was without doubt suitable for its time. But the old court fee rates are in no way adequate to meet the judiciary's needsgiven the current inflation rate. )
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
