549 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 194 0 R
/E (Furthermore, this proclamation is unprecedented in detailing explicit responsibilities for entities that control and manage personal data \(Art. 42-52\). These entities are mandated to secure informed consent for the use of personal data \(Art 48\), enforce suitable security protocols, and report any data breaches \(Art 43\). They are also bound by restrictions, such as collecting data solely for essential business operations.)
/Pg 40 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
550 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 194 0 R
/E (Consequently, businesses are obligated to collect and utilize data for legitimate purposes only and to maintain transparency with users regarding their data management practices.)
/Pg 40 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
551 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 194 0 R
/E (The proclamation also addresses Data Security Measures Art. 42\(a\), underscoring the importance of data protection through strategies like encryption Art.17\(4\)\(a\) and limitations on data transfers. It requires data controllers to establish defenses against unauthorized access, exposure, or loss of personal data Art 28\(3\)\(d\), Art. 29\(1\), and Art. 30 \(1\)\(b\).)
/Pg 40 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
552 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 194 0 R
/E (In a move that has surprised many, the proclamation also includes stipulations on Cross-Border Transfers. It stipulates that personal data cannot be transferred to nations without sufficient data protection laws unless specific criteria are satisfied. These criteria include: )
/Pg 40 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
